WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.260
SpaceX is renaming its ai unit to space xsi, while scrubbing an sda satellite launch and prepping

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:15.640
starship ship 43 for a possible Florida debut. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 121. Cryogenic

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:21.460
proof testing for ships 42 and 43 may begin within days after the ship thrust simulator

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:28.400
stand returned to starbase. Ship 43 is stacked inside megabay 2 and could become the first starship

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.880
to launch from LC-39A at Kennedy Space Center.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:38.440
A provisional no earlier than date of October 30th appears in an FAA scheduling document.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.540
The vehicle began stacking on August 20th and is now inside Megabade 2 at Starbase in

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:45.200
South Texas.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:52.340
Flight 16 is currently assigned to the Starlink 30-1 mission, marking one of the first operational

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.120
Starlink payloads on Starship, according to BaseNor.com.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:01.780
One thing worth watching is that Starship must complete a full set of orbital refueling

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.920
and lunar lander demonstrations before NASA's Artemis IV crew landing target in early

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.060
2028.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:16.020
The vehicle has flown only 14 times since 2023 and still faces heat shield, engine and refueling

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.880
milestones that must be cleared on a compressed schedule per BGR.com.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:27.260
An automated start-up sequence triggered a hold on booster B-1103's sixth flight during

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.340
the SDA mission.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.020
The Tranch-1 Transport Layer A mission for the Space Development Agency was scrubbed during

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.240
automated start-up.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.640
The Falcon 9 with booster B-1103 on its sixth flight is now targeted for no earlier than

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.060
Tuesday, October 6th, at 117 AM Pacific Daylight Time from Vandenberg.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:52.480
The booster is planned to land at landing zone 4 for the 37th time at that site.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.780
The 21 satellites were built by Northrop Grumman and formed the 4th production batch for

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.820
the proliferated warfighter space architecture, according to spaceflightnow.com.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:08.460
Musk confirmed the change on X after a user suggested switching from SpaceX AI.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.620
He replied, yes, we will make that change.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:18.000
after posting that there should be no more AI and describing space X as a super intelligence company.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:24.640
The move would mark the second rebrand in months following the absorption of XAI earlier this year.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.540
Trademark filings from the prior change already covered satellite data centers, cloud computing,

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.740
model training, and AI workload management.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:41.680
Observers on X noted that the SpaceX AI-to-space XSI rebrand aligns with a White House executive

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:48.100
order directing federal agencies to replace AI terminology with superintelligence per

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:54.540
tesla-radi.com. Reflections sign a 150 million dollars per month compute rental agreement

00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:01.960
with SpaceX. The deal provides immediate access to NVIDIA GB300 chips at Colossus 2, beginning

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:06.360
joining July 1st, 2026, and runs through 2029.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.100
Either party can exit with 90 days notice after the initial three months.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.660
The full term would total roughly $6.3 billion.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.800
Reflection is preparing to release an open-weight model, expected to compete with leading Chinese

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.200
offerings, according to 4WeekMBA.com.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:30.860
Blue Origin is rebuilding Launch Complex 36 after a May anomaly, and still aims to return

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:36.740
new Glenn to flight by year end. Relativity has hardware in integrated build for Terran R,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:43.480
with a late 2026 Cape target that observers expect to slip. Rocket Labs' neutron window for

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:51.080
2026 is narrowing toward a 2027 debut. New Glenn is rated for roughly 45 tons to low-earth orbit

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:59.260
in reusable configuration. Terran R targets 23.5 tons reusable, and 33.5 tons expendable,

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.480
according to nextbigfuture.com.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.820
The engineering reality behind the final minute scrub centers on the Falcon 9's automated

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:12.180
start-up sequence, which begins roughly 60 seconds before liftoff and hands primary

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.660
control to the flight computer.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.540
At that point, the vehicle checks propellant pressures, valve positions, and engine health

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:24.560
in a rapid cascade that must all report nominal before the hold-down clamps release.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.280
A single out of tolerance reading, whether from a sensor glitch, a valve response time,

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:36.040
or a ground system handoff, triggers an immediate abort to protect the booster and the 21

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.100
Northrop Grumman satellites on board.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:45.940
The sequence is deliberately conservative because the same first stage booster, B1103, is already

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:51.460
on its sixth flight and will attempt its 37th landing at LZ4 if the mission flies.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:59.240
That reuse-driven reliability requirement forces tighter margins on every ground check than an expendable vehicle would tolerate.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:07.780
The net effect is that a scrubbed attempt today simply shifts the same hardware stack to the next available window without consuming a flight article,

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:13.380
preserving the cadence that has already delivered three missions in 13 hours earlier this week.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:19.980
The Falcon 9 first stage uses 9 Merlin 1D engines that must reach stable chamber pressure

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.460
within a narrow time window before the vehicle commits to flight.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:29.320
Each engine carries its own set of turbo pump speed sensors and propellant valve position

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.820
encoders that feed the flight computer in parallel.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:38.100
When any parameter deviates beyond preset limits, the computer issues an immediate hold and

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.220
the ground systems vent residual pressure.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:45.960
This layered check prevents a partial ignition or valve misalignment from propagating into

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.000
a more serious anomaly once the vehicle is committed.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:55.000
The approach trades a small amount of schedule margin for the ability to reuse the same booster

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.480
dozens of times without cumulative damage from marginal starts.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:04.560
Because the booster has already flown five prior missions, the ground team can compare today's

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.620
sensor traces against the historical baseline for that specific vehicle, tightening the tolerance

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:15.380
bands further. The result is a launch system that can absorb a late abort, recycle the countdown

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:21.120
within 24 hours, and still meet the aggressive cadence demanded by both commercial and national

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:28.780
security customers. A quick look at the tape shows SPCX moving roughly flat. Before we go, the next

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:34.920
window for the SDA satellites sits less than 48 hours out. That covers everything worth knowing

